Aim: To develop a simple JavaScript application and perform automated API testing using the Karate framework.

We will create a simple Calculator API using JavaScript and Node.js, then test it using Karate.

Requirements:
- Node.js
- Java JDK
- Maven
- Karate Framework
- VS Code
Step 1: Create a project
Create a folder named KarateJavaScript.
KarateJavaScript/
 ├── server.js
 └── karate-tests/
      ├── pom.xml
      └── src/
           └── test/
                └── java/
                     ├── CalculatorTest.java
                     └── calculator.feature


Step 2: Write the JavaScript program
Create server.js.
const express = require('express');

const app = express();

app.use(express.json());

app.post('/add', (req, res) => {

    const a = req.body.a;
    const b = req.body.b;

    const result = a + b;

    res.json({
        operation: "addition",
        result: result
    });
});

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
    res.send("Calculator API is running");
});

app.listen(3000, () => {
    console.log("Server running on port 3000");
});


Step 3: Install Node.js dependencies
Open the terminal in the project folder.
npm init -y


Install Express:
npm install express


Step 4: Run the JavaScript application
node server.js


Output:
Server running on port 3000


Step 5: Test manually
Send a POST request to:
http://localhost:3000/add


Input (JSON):
{
    "a": 10,
    "b": 20
}


Output:
{
    "operation": "addition",
    "result": 30
}


Step 6: Create Maven pom.xml
Inside the karate-tests folder, create pom.xml.
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <artifactId>karate-tests</artifactId>
    <version>1.0</version>

    <properties>
        <maven.compiler.release>17</maven.compiler.release>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <karate.version>1.4.1</karate.version>
        <junit.version>5.10.2</junit.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.intuit.karate</groupId>
            <artifactId>karate-junit5</artifactId>
            <version>${karate.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <version>${junit.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.2.5</version>
                <configuration>
                    <includes>
                        <include>**/*Test.java</include>
                    </includes>
                    <systemPropertyVariables>
                        <karate.options>--tags ~@ignore</karate.options>
                    </systemPropertyVariables>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>


Step 7: Write Karate test cases
Create calculator.feature inside src/test/java.
Feature: Calculator API Testing

Background:
    * url 'http://localhost:3000'

Scenario: Test addition of two numbers
    Given path 'add'
    And request { a: 10, b: 20 }
    When method POST
    Then status 200
    And match response.result == 30
    And match response.operation == 'addition'

Scenario: Test addition with zero
    Given path 'add'
    And request { a: 5, b: 0 }
    When method POST
    Then status 200
    And match response.result == 5

Scenario: Test API home page
    Given path '/'
    When method GET
    Then status 200
    And match response == 'Calculator API is running'


Step 8: Create Karate test runner
Create CalculatorTest.java:
import com.intuit.karate.junit5.Karate;

class CalculatorTest {

    @Karate.Test
    Karate testCalculator() {
        return Karate.run("calculator");
    }
}


Step 9: Execute Karate tests
Keep the JavaScript server running in the first terminal.

Open another terminal and enter the karate-tests folder.

Execute:
mvn test


Expected output
Scenario: Test addition of two numbers
Scenario: Test addition with zero
Scenario: Test API home page

Tests run: 3
Failures: 0
Errors: 0

BUILD SUCCESS


Explanation of Karate keywords
Keyword	Meaning
Feature	Describes the feature being tested
Background	Common setup for scenarios
Scenario	Individual test case
Given	Initial condition
When	Action performed
Then	Expected result
And	Additional condition
url	Specifies API URL
path	Specifies API endpoint
request	Sends request data
method	Executes HTTP method
status	Checks HTTP status
match	Compares actual and expected values

Result: Successfully developed a JavaScript Calculator API and performed automated API testing using Karate Framework.
